     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order as written. The FDA-approved labeling doesn't support the claim that it's within the maximum.\n\n- **Immediate-release metformin:** the maximum is 2,550 mg per day, and no more than 1,000 mg in a single dose in most labeling. The usual dosing is 850 mg three times daily.\n- **Extended-release metformin:** the maximum is 2,000\u20132,500 mg per day, depending on the product.\n- **This order:** 1,700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg per day, about double the labeled maximum.\n\nI can't verify the label myself or any approval for an exception. Given the lactic acidosis risk, I won't enter a dose this far above the maximum on that basis.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- Enter **850 mg PO three times daily with meals**, which is 2,550 mg per day.\n- Enter any other dose within the labeled range.\n- Add a note to the order if there's a specific clinical reason for the dose.\n\nIf you believe a dose above the label is clinically necessary, please review it with pharmacy or the attending. They can place it through the hospital's override process. Tell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
